-- title:
--   Membership in ⟨ Γ, -1⟩ inside SL₂(ℤ)
-- statement:
--   Let $\Gamma$ be a subgroup of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$, written `SL(2, ℤ)` in the `MatrixGroups` notation, and let $g$ be an element of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$. The assertion is that $g$ belongs to the join $\Gamma \sqcup \langle -1 \rangle$ in the lattice of subgroups, where $\langle -1\rangle$ is `Subgroup.zpowers (-1)`, the subgroup of integer powers of the scalar matrix $-1$, if and only if $g \in \Gamma$ or $-g \in \Gamma$. Equivalently, the subgroup generated by $\Gamma$ together with $-1$ is, as a set, the union $\Gamma \cup (-1)\Gamma$. No hypothesis is placed on $\Gamma$ beyond being a subgroup; in particular $\Gamma$ need not contain $-1$, and when it does the two sides of the disjunction coincide.
--
--   This is the standard identification of $\pm\Gamma = \langle \Gamma, -1\rangle$ with $\Gamma \cup (-1)\Gamma$, used to pass between $\Gamma$-equivalence and $\pm\Gamma$-equivalence of points of the upper half-plane and between the corresponding stabilisers and indices. It is invoked in the modular-curve layer, for instance in the index computations for $\Gamma_H$ at full level and in the finiteness and rank bounds for the algebras adjoined to Laurent base changes.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Matrix_SpecialLinearGroup_mem_sup_zpowers_neg_one_iff.lean

import Mathlib

set_option maxHeartbeats 4000000
